---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="10 Common JSON Errors and How to Fix Them (2026 Guide)"
    description="Learn the most common JSON syntax errors developers make and how to fix them fast. Includes real examples and a free online JSON validator."
>
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <header class="mb-8">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-2">Published: October 9, 2026</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">
                10 Common JSON Errors and How to Fix Them (2026 Guide)
            </h1>
            <p class="text-lg text-gray-600">
                I've debugged hundreds of broken JSON files. These are the ten
                errors that show up again and again — and the fastest way to fix
                each one.
            </p>
        </header>

        <section class="space-y-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
                JSON looks simple. So simple that beginners assume they can't
                get it wrong. Then they paste a config into an API tool, hit
                "Send," and get back an error that says "Unexpected token at
                position 347" — with no hint about what's actually broken.
            </p>
            <p>
                I've been there. After years of working with APIs, config files,
                and data pipelines, I've made every JSON mistake in the book.
                This guide covers the top ten — with exact examples and fixes.
                If you're stuck right now, paste your JSON into our{" "}
                <a
                    href="/tools/json-formatter"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    free JSON Formatter
                </a>{" "}
                and it'll show you exactly where the error is.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Quick Answer: Most JSON Errors Are One of These Three
            </h2>
            <p>
                If you don't want to read all ten, here's the short version: 80%
                of JSON errors come from <strong>trailing commas</strong>, <strong
                    >single quotes</strong
                >, or
                <strong>unquoted keys</strong>. Fix those three habits and
                you'll eliminate most of your debugging time.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">1. Trailing Commas</h2>
            <p>
                This is the #1 JSON error I see. JavaScript allows trailing
                commas in objects and arrays; JSON does not.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
                <div class="p-4 bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-red-900 mb-1">❌ Invalid:</p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-red-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{
  "name": "Ali",
  "age": 25,
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="p-4 bg-green-50 border border-green-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-green-900 mb-1">✅ Valid:</p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-green-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{
  "name": "Ali",
  "age": 25
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
                <strong>Fix:</strong> Remove the comma after the last item in every
                object and array. Most modern editors catch this, but if you're hand-editing
                a config file, it's easy to miss.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                2. Single Quotes Instead of Double Quotes
            </h2>
            <p>
                JavaScript accepts both <code>'single'</code> and <code
                    >"double"</code
                > quotes. JSON only accepts double quotes.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
                <div class="p-4 bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-red-900 mb-1">❌ Invalid:</p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-red-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{ 'name': 'Ali' }`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="p-4 bg-green-50 border border-green-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-green-900 mb-1">✅ Valid:</p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-green-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{ "name": "Ali" }`}</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
                <strong>Fix:</strong> Replace all single quotes with double quotes.
                If you're converting from a JavaScript object, use <code
                    >JSON.stringify()</code
                > instead of manual editing.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">3. Unquoted Keys</h2>
            <p>
                In JavaScript, object keys don't need quotes. In JSON, they do.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
                <div class="p-4 bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-red-900 mb-1">❌ Invalid:</p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-red-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{ name: "Ali", age: 25 }`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="p-4 bg-green-50 border border-green-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-green-900 mb-1">✅ Valid:</p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-green-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{ "name": "Ali", "age": 25 }`}</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
                <strong>Fix:</strong> Quote every key with double quotes. No exceptions.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">4. Comments in JSON</h2>
            <p>
                JSON does not support comments — not <code>//</code>, not <code
                    >/* */</code
                >, not <code>#</code>. If you need comments, you're not using
                JSON anymore (you're using JSONC, JSON5, or YAML).
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
                <div class="p-4 bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-red-900 mb-1">❌ Invalid:</p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-red-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{
  // user info
  "name": "Ali"
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
                <strong>Fix:</strong> Remove comments. If you need documentation,
                store it in a separate file or use JSONC (which supports comments
                but isn't standard JSON).
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                5. Using <code>undefined</code>
            </h2>
            <p>
                JavaScript has <code>undefined</code>; JSON doesn't. Use <code
                    >null</code
                >
                instead.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
                <div class="p-4 bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-red-900 mb-1">❌ Invalid:</p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-red-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{ "middleName": undefined }`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="p-4 bg-green-50 border border-green-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-green-900 mb-1">✅ Valid:</p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-green-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{ "middleName": null }`}</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                6. Numbers in Quotes (When They Shouldn't Be)
            </h2>
            <p>
                <code>"25"</code> (string) and <code>25</code> (number) are different
                types. If your API expects a number and gets a string, it breaks.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
                <div class="p-4 bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-red-900 mb-1">
                        ❌ Wrong type:
                    </p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-red-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{ "age": "25" }`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="p-4 bg-green-50 border border-green-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-green-900 mb-1">
                        ✅ Correct type:
                    </p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-green-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{ "age": 25 }`}</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                7. Unescaped Special Characters
            </h2>
            <p>
                Newlines, tabs, and quotes inside strings must be escaped with a
                backslash. Otherwise JSON breaks.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
                <div class="p-4 bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-red-900 mb-1">❌ Invalid:</p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-red-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{ "message": "He said "hello"" }`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="p-4 bg-green-50 border border-green-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-green-900 mb-1">✅ Valid:</p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-green-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{ "message": "He said \\"hello\\"" }`}</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
                <strong>Common escapes:</strong>
                <code>\"</code> (quote), <code>\\</code>
                (backslash), <code>\n</code> (newline), <code>\t</code> (tab).
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                8. Missing Closing Brackets
            </h2>
            <p>
                When you have deeply nested objects and arrays, it's easy to
                lose track of which bracket closes what.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
                <div class="p-4 bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-lg">
                    <p class="font-semibold text-red-900 mb-1">
                        ❌ Invalid (missing <code>{"}"}</code>):
                    </p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-red-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{
  "user": {
    "name": "Ali",
    "scores": [90, 85, 88]
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
                <strong>Fix:</strong> Format your JSON with proper indentation. Our{
                    " "
                }
                <a
                    href="/tools/json-formatter"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    JSON Formatter
                </a>{" "}
                auto-indents and shows exactly where the error is.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">9. Duplicate Keys</h2>
            <p>
                JSON technically allows duplicate keys, but most parsers take
                the last value. This leads to silent bugs — the worst kind.
            </p>
            <div class="mt-4 space-y-3">
                <div
                    class="p-4 bg-yellow-50 border border-yellow-200 rounded-lg"
                >
                    <p class="font-semibold text-yellow-900 mb-1">
                        ⚠️ Technically valid, but dangerous:
                    </p>
                    <pre
                        class="text-sm text-yellow-800 overflow-x-auto"><code>{`{
  "name": "Ali",
  "name": "Ahmed"
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3">
                <strong>Fix:</strong> Never use duplicate keys. If you need multiple
                values, use an array.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                10. Trailing Whitespace and Invisible Characters
            </h2>
            <p>
                If you copy JSON from a Word document or a chat app, you might
                bring in invisible characters — non-breaking spaces, BOM
                markers, zero-width spaces. These aren't visible but break JSON
                parsing.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Fix:</strong> Paste your JSON into a plain text editor (like
                Notepad or VS Code with "show invisibles" enabled) to spot them. Our{
                    " "
                }
                <a
                    href="/tools/json-formatter"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    JSON Formatter
                </a>{" "}
                strips them automatically when it validates.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                The Fastest Way to Debug Any JSON Error
            </h2>
            <p>Here's the workflow I use when JSON breaks:</p>
            <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
                <li>
                    Paste the entire JSON into our{" "}
                    <a
                        href="/tools/json-formatter"
                        class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                    >
                        free JSON Formatter
                    </a>
                </li>
                <li>
                    Click <strong>"Format"</strong> — if there's an error, it shows
                    exactly where
                </li>
                <li>Fix the error and re-check</li>
                <li>Once valid, the tool beautifies it for easier reading</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                This is faster than reading through hundreds of lines of JSON
                trying to spot a missing comma. I use it every day.
            </p>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">
                Frequently Asked Questions
            </h2>
            <div class="space-y-4 mt-3">
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Why does my JSON work in JavaScript but fail in an API?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        JavaScript is lenient — it accepts single quotes,
                        trailing commas, and unquoted keys. JSON is strict. Use <code
                            >JSON.stringify()</code
                        > to convert a JavaScript object into valid JSON.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        What's the fastest way to find a JSON error?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Paste it into our{" "}
                        <a
                            href="/tools/json-formatter"
                            class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                            >JSON Formatter</a
                        >. It shows the exact line and character where the
                        syntax breaks.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Can I use comments in JSON?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        No. Standard JSON has no comment syntax. Use JSONC if
                        you need comments — but note that most APIs won't accept
                        JSONC.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Why does my JSON have a BOM at the beginning?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Some text editors save files with a Byte Order Mark
                        (BOM) by default. This is invisible but breaks JSON
                        parsing. In VS Code, save as "UTF-8 without BOM."
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
                        Is there a JSON linter for VS Code?
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-1">
                        Yes. VS Code has built-in JSON validation, and
                        extensions like "JSON Formatter" add more features. For
                        quick one-off checks, our online tool is faster.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Final Thoughts</h2>
            <p>
                JSON errors are frustrating because they're small — a single
                comma, a quote character, an unquoted key. But once you know the
                ten most common mistakes, you can spot them in seconds.
            </p>
            <p>
                If you're stuck right now, don't waste time reading through your
                file manually. Paste it into our{" "}
                <a
                    href="/tools/json-formatter"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium"
                >
                    free JSON Formatter
                </a>{" "}
                and let it tell you exactly where the error is. For a beginner's introduction
                to JSON itself, see my{" "}
                <a
                    href="/blog/json-explained"
                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                >
                    JSON explained guide
                </a>.
            </p>
        </section>

        <footer class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-200">
            <a href="/blog" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline font-medium">
                ← Back to Blog
            </a>
        </footer>
    </article>
</Layout>
